1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,539
Part Four introduces containers using Apptainer.

2
00:00:02,539 --> 00:00:07,193
Containers package an application and its libraries into a portable image.

3
00:00:07,193 --> 00:00:13,963
On RCC, the operational reason is especially important: a large Conda environment contains thousands of small

4
00:00:13,963 --> 00:00:14,387
files.

5
00:00:14,387 --> 00:00:19,464
If those files are repeatedly opened from network storage by many jobs,

6
00:00:19,464 --> 00:00:22,426
metadata and random input-output can dominate runtime.

7
00:00:22,426 --> 00:00:29,196
A read-only Apptainer S I F image consolidates that software stack into one managed artifact and

8
00:00:29,196 --> 00:00:31,735
is suitable for shared high-performance computing.

9
00:00:31,735 --> 00:00:37,236
We will distinguish the image from project data, explain cache and temporary storage,

10
00:00:37,236 --> 00:00:44,006
cover bind mounts and GPU access, and show how Snakemake associates a container with a rule.

11
00:00:44,006 --> 00:00:49,432
A Conda environment can contain tens of thousands of files: Python modules,

12
00:00:49,432 --> 00:00:53,049
shared libraries, metadata records, executables, and package caches.

13
00:00:53,049 --> 00:01:00,284
Starting a command may trigger many opens, stats, library searches, and imports before useful computation begins.

14
00:01:00,284 --> 00:01:03,901
On local solid-state storage this may be acceptable.

15
00:01:03,901 --> 00:01:11,135
On shared network storage, hundreds of simultaneous jobs can turn environment startup into a metadata bottleneck.

16
00:01:11,135 --> 00:01:17,465
The result may look like slow software even though the CPU is mostly waiting.

17
00:01:17,465 --> 00:01:24,700
Containers address this by packaging the software stack into a smaller number of image artifacts and

18
00:01:24,700 --> 00:01:27,865
by making the environment immutable and reproducible.

19
00:01:27,865 --> 00:01:32,144
A container image packages user-space software, libraries, and selected configuration.

20
00:01:32,144 --> 00:01:38,564
It does not include a separate hardware machine and normally shares the host Linux kernel.

21
00:01:38,564 --> 00:01:43,272
Apptainer is designed for multi-user high-performance computing and normally runs rootless.

22
00:01:43,272 --> 00:01:50,119
That means the process keeps your RCC user and group access instead of gaining unrestricted root

23
00:01:50,119 --> 00:01:55,683
on the host, and it does not depend on a privileged Docker-style daemon.

24
00:01:55,683 --> 00:02:00,819
This is important on shared compute nodes: the host kernel, drivers, devices,

25
00:02:00,819 --> 00:02:07,666
file permissions, and Slurm allocation remain under RCC control, while a mistaken or compromised application is

26
00:02:07,666 --> 00:02:11,946
limited to the access already held by the submitting user.

27
00:02:11,946 --> 00:02:16,226
Rootless does not mean harmless or isolated from your data.

28
00:02:16,226 --> 00:02:22,646
The container can still read, change, or delete visible files that your user can access,

29
00:02:22,646 --> 00:02:29,065
so bind only the paths the job needs and make source data read-only where possible.

30
00:02:29,065 --> 00:02:35,485
The image is not the correct place for active project data, credentials, or patient information.

31
00:02:35,485 --> 00:02:41,048
Containers improve reproducibility of software, but they do not validate algorithms, reference data,

32
00:02:41,048 --> 00:02:42,760
parameters, or scientific interpretation.

33
00:02:42,760 --> 00:02:48,324
Building an image is a separate operation and must use the RCC-approved rootless,

34
00:02:48,324 --> 00:02:50,036
fakeroot, or remote-builder pattern.

35
00:02:50,036 --> 00:02:54,465
Apptainer commonly uses the Singularity Image Format, or S I F.

36
00:02:54,465 --> 00:02:59,698
A SIF image can contain a compressed read-only filesystem plus metadata and signatures.

37
00:02:59,698 --> 00:03:06,140
Read-only images reduce accidental drift: a job cannot silently install a different library into the image.

38
00:03:06,140 --> 00:03:09,763
The same image can be used by many jobs.

39
00:03:09,763 --> 00:03:14,595
Record the image source, digest or checksum, creation recipe, and validation status.

40
00:03:14,595 --> 00:03:19,023
Do not rely only on a floating tag such as latest.

41
00:03:19,023 --> 00:03:25,465
A container is a versioned research dependency and should be managed with the same care as

42
00:03:25,465 --> 00:03:27,880
a reference genome or analysis script.

43
00:03:27,880 --> 00:03:30,559
Container workflows involve several storage locations.

44
00:03:30,559 --> 00:03:34,576
The SIF image is a durable, versioned software artifact.

45
00:03:34,576 --> 00:03:39,933
The Apptainer cache stores downloaded layers and should use an RCC-approved path;

46
00:03:39,933 --> 00:03:46,182
uncontrolled caches on a shared home directory can consume space and create metadata traffic.

47
00:03:46,182 --> 00:03:51,093
Temporary build or extraction space should use local scratch when possible.

48
00:03:51,093 --> 00:03:56,896
Project data remains in approved shared storage and is bound into the container.

49
00:03:56,896 --> 00:04:04,038
Distinguishing these locations prevents accidental data loss and avoids turning network storage into a scratch disk.

50
00:04:04,038 --> 00:04:11,180
The RCC should publish standard environment variables and paths for image stores, caches, and temporary directories.

51
00:04:11,180 --> 00:04:16,881
Only run images from trusted sources or images built from reviewed definitions.

52
00:04:16,881 --> 00:04:19,732
Prefer immutable digests over floating tags.

53
00:04:19,732 --> 00:04:25,908
Calculate and record a checksum for a SIF image when it is transferred.

54
00:04:25,908 --> 00:04:28,283
Inspect image metadata and labels.

55
00:04:28,283 --> 00:04:33,985
Record the base image, installed package versions, build date, and definition file.

56
00:04:33,985 --> 00:04:37,785
Scan or review images according to institutional policy.

57
00:04:37,785 --> 00:04:43,011
Most importantly, validate the scientific behavior with synthetic and reference data.

58
00:04:43,011 --> 00:04:50,613
A correctly signed image can still contain an unsuitable algorithm, outdated database, or incorrect default parameter.

59
00:04:50,613 --> 00:04:55,364
Trust has both a software-supply-chain component and a scientific-validation component.

60
00:04:55,364 --> 00:04:58,875
Apptainer exec runs a command inside an image.

61
00:04:58,875 --> 00:05:03,704
Start with a harmless version check, then a small synthetic test.

62
00:05:03,704 --> 00:05:09,849
The clean environment option reduces accidental inheritance of host variables that could change behavior.

63
00:05:09,849 --> 00:05:15,116
The contain-all or site-recommended isolation options may be appropriate depending on policy.

64
00:05:15,116 --> 00:05:18,628
Record the full command in the workflow log.

65
00:05:18,628 --> 00:05:25,651
Avoid interactive changes inside writable sandboxes for production work because those changes are difficult to reproduce.

66
00:05:25,651 --> 00:05:30,918
If a command depends on locale, threads, temporary paths, or license variables,

67
00:05:30,918 --> 00:05:34,868
declare them explicitly and document why they are needed.

68
00:05:34,868 --> 00:05:41,371
A bind mount makes a host path visible at a chosen path inside the container.

69
00:05:41,371 --> 00:05:43,971
Bind source data read-only whenever possible.

70
00:05:43,971 --> 00:05:46,572
Bind a dedicated result directory read-write.

71
00:05:46,572 --> 00:05:49,173
Bind node-local scratch for temporary work.

72
00:05:49,173 --> 00:05:56,109
Avoid binding your entire home directory merely for convenience, because that makes hidden configuration and credentials

73
00:05:56,109 --> 00:05:57,843
visible and reduces reproducibility.

74
00:05:57,843 --> 00:06:04,779
Use stable internal paths such as slash data, slash results, and slash tmp so the command

75
00:06:04,779 --> 00:06:07,813
is independent of the host project layout.

76
00:06:07,813 --> 00:06:11,714
Verify ownership and permissions before starting thousands of jobs.

77
00:06:11,714 --> 00:06:15,675
A container does not make random network input-output fast.

78
00:06:15,675 --> 00:06:22,717
The executable may be packaged efficiently, but inputs, outputs, caches, and temporary files still follow the

79
00:06:22,717 --> 00:06:24,037
underlying storage behavior.

80
00:06:24,037 --> 00:06:26,678
Keep compressed inputs compressed when supported.

81
00:06:26,678 --> 00:06:32,399
Stage files that require random access or many temporary writes to node-local scratch.

82
00:06:32,399 --> 00:06:37,240
Avoid writing package caches or application caches into the project directory.

83
00:06:37,240 --> 00:06:41,641
Limit the number of tiny output files and organize directories.

84
00:06:41,641 --> 00:06:48,682
A container can even make performance worse if every job independently pulls an image from a

85
00:06:48,682 --> 00:06:49,122
registry.

86
00:06:49,122 --> 00:06:55,724
Pre-stage approved images and use a shared read-only image location or an RCC-supported cache strategy.

87
00:06:55,724 --> 00:07:02,403
For a GPU job, request the GPU through Slurm and run Apptainer with the N V

88
00:07:02,403 --> 00:07:08,665
option so the relevant NVIDIA devices and host driver libraries are available inside the container.

89
00:07:08,665 --> 00:07:14,092
The image should contain compatible user-space frameworks such as CUDA-enabled PyTorch or TensorFlow,

90
00:07:14,092 --> 00:07:18,684
but it should not attempt to replace the host kernel driver.

91
00:07:18,684 --> 00:07:24,946
Test compatibility on a small allocation and use nvidia-smi both on the host allocation and,

92
00:07:24,946 --> 00:07:27,033
where appropriate, inside the container.

93
00:07:27,033 --> 00:07:31,207
Record image digest, GPU type, driver, framework version, and command.

94
00:07:31,207 --> 00:07:37,052
GPU access does not change the need to optimize data loading and batch size.

95
00:07:37,052 --> 00:07:43,366
Snakemake can associate a container image with a rule and execute the workflow using the Apptainer

96
00:07:43,366 --> 00:07:44,156
deployment method.

97
00:07:44,156 --> 00:07:50,470
This places the software dependency next to the declared inputs, outputs, threads, resources, log, and command.

98
00:07:50,470 --> 00:07:56,390
Use an RCC-supported Apptainer prefix or image store so jobs do not repeatedly download images.

99
00:07:56,390 --> 00:08:02,705
The rule should still use tmpdir or a shadow pattern when the tool needs local temporary

100
00:08:02,705 --> 00:08:03,100
work.

101
00:08:03,100 --> 00:08:08,625
Pin the image to an immutable digest or version and record it in reports.

102
00:08:08,625 --> 00:08:13,756
Test both the workflow logic and the image before scaling to production data.

103
00:08:13,756 --> 00:08:20,711
A production image should be built from a version-controlled Apptainer definition file or an equivalent reviewed

104
00:08:20,711 --> 00:08:21,146
recipe.

105
00:08:21,146 --> 00:08:27,666
Build in an approved environment, because image construction may require privileges or a remote builder.

106
00:08:27,666 --> 00:08:30,274
Record base images and package versions.

107
00:08:30,274 --> 00:08:35,491
Run automated smoke tests, workflow tests with synthetic data, and scientific validation.

108
00:08:35,491 --> 00:08:41,142
Publish the image to an approved registry or image store, record the digest,

109
00:08:41,142 --> 00:08:42,881
and make it read-only.

110
00:08:42,881 --> 00:08:47,228
Rebuild through the recipe rather than modifying an existing image.

111
00:08:47,228 --> 00:08:53,748
Deprecate images that contain security problems or obsolete references, but preserve provenance for completed analyses.

112
00:08:53,748 --> 00:08:56,253
The container rules are these.

113
00:08:56,253 --> 00:08:58,758
Use trusted, immutable SIF images.

114
00:08:58,758 --> 00:09:02,264
Record source, digest, build recipe, and validation.

115
00:09:02,264 --> 00:09:05,270
Keep project data outside the image.

116
00:09:05,270 --> 00:09:11,281
Bind source data read-only, results deliberately, and temporary space to local scratch.

117
00:09:11,281 --> 00:09:14,788
Place caches and images in RCC-approved locations.

118
00:09:14,788 --> 00:09:17,793
Use clean environments and explicit commands.

119
00:09:17,793 --> 00:09:24,806
Request GPUs through Slurm and use the Apptainer GPU option only for compatible images.

120
00:09:24,806 --> 00:09:31,319
In Snakemake, pin the container per rule or workflow and avoid repeated pulls.

121
00:09:31,319 --> 00:09:38,332
Remember that containers improve software reproducibility and startup I/O, but good data input-output patterns,

122
00:09:38,332 --> 00:09:41,839
performance measurement, and scientific validation remain essential.

